# =============================================================================
# data-migration/.dockerignore
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Purpose:
#   Excludes paths from the build context of data-migration/Dockerfile. That
#   image declares THIS directory as its context -- its own header states the
#   build command is run from here -- so the daemon resolves this file from here
#   and the repository-root .dockerignore has no effect on it. The two files are
#   siblings in purpose and neither substitutes for the other.
#
# Parameters (the inputs this file governs):
#   One input: the set of paths the daemon receives when a build is started from
#   data-migration/. Patterns are matched relative to this directory. A pattern
#   removes a path from the context entirely, so a removed path cannot be reached
#   by any COPY instruction and cannot appear in any layer.
#
# Return values:
#   None directly. Its effect is observable as the "transferring context" size a
#   build reports and as the absence of the excluded paths from every layer.
#
# Exceptions or errors:
#   Excluding something the Dockerfile copies fails the build immediately on a
#   missing path. The four things it copies are requirements.txt,
#   requirements-build.txt, pyproject.toml, src/ and sql/, and none of them is
#   matched by any pattern below.
#
# Governing convention:
#   docs/CODE_DOCUMENTATION_STANDARD.md
#
# WHY (non-obvious design decisions):
#   1. Assumptions: this context is already narrow, so the exclusions here are
#      about correctness rather than size. The directory holds no build output of
#      its own to shadow, but it DOES accumulate interpreter caches next to the
#      source the image installs, and a __pycache__ copied into the builder holds
#      bytecode compiled by whichever interpreter the host ran. The image pins its
#      own interpreter precisely so that the codecs behave identically to the
#      reference test harness; admitting host-compiled bytecode would undermine
#      the one property the pin exists for.
#   2. Refactoring Rationale: the test tree is excluded even though it is
#      committed and useful, because the image is a runtime artifact and the
#      Dockerfile copies only the package and the schema. Leaving tests/ in the
#      context would transfer fixtures the image never installs, and a fixture is
#      a record layout that resembles production data closely enough that its
#      presence in an image layer would have to be reasoned about. Excluding it
#      removes the question.
#   3. Trade-offs: requirements-dev.txt is excluded while requirements.txt and
#      requirements-build.txt are not. The Dockerfile installs the latter two by
#      name and never the first, so excluding it costs nothing and states that the
#      development closure is not an image input -- which the Dockerfile's own
#      comment about a separate never-copied closure already asserts and this
#      makes true by construction rather than by discipline.
# =============================================================================

# WHY : Assumptions: local environment files are excluded by pattern rather than
#       by name, because the risk is the file nobody remembered -- an untracked
#       .env holding a real database password sitting beside the package that
#       connects with one. No .env is committed here and none is copied; the
#       pattern exists so that a developer's own file cannot become an image
#       input.
.env
.env.*

# WHY : Assumptions: interpreter and tool caches are machine-local and can shadow
#       what the image installs, for the reason recorded in item 1 above. An
#       editable install performed on the host also leaves path-bearing metadata
#       behind, which is broken inside a container in a way that is confusing to
#       diagnose.
__pycache__
**/__pycache__
*.pyc
*.pyo
.venv
**/.venv
*.egg-info
**/*.egg-info
.pytest_cache
**/.pytest_cache
.ruff_cache
**/.ruff_cache
.mypy_cache
**/.mypy_cache
.coverage
htmlcov

# WHY : Refactoring Rationale: the test tree and the development closure are
#       excluded because the image installs neither, for the reasons recorded in
#       items 2 and 3 above. Nothing in the Dockerfile reaches for either, so a
#       build cannot break on their absence -- and if a future edit needs one, the
#       correct response is to remove the pattern deliberately rather than to
#       discover it had been available all along.
tests
requirements-dev.txt

# WHY : Trade-offs: temporary validation artifacts are excluded by their agreed
#       prefix. They are never committed, so this rule only matters for a build
#       run from a dirty tree -- which is precisely the situation in which an
#       unexpected file reaching a layer would be hardest to notice.
blitzy_adhoc_test_*
**/blitzy_adhoc_test_*

# WHY : Assumptions: editor and operating-system metadata is excluded last
#       because it is the least consequential of these categories, not because it
#       is harmless: an editor directory can carry local tool settings and
#       occasionally credentials for a language server or a database client.
.idea
.vscode
*.swp
.DS_Store
